# Copy this file to features/<site>/<name>.feature (drop ".example") and edit.
# Rules:
#  1. Tag the feature with the site's exact name (same as config/<site>.properties):
#     that is how -Dsite=<site> picks which features run.
#  2. Add ONE type tag so the hooks start the right thing:
#       @ui      browser          @mobile   Appium session          @api   no driver
#  3. Elements are inline locators or object-repository keys:
#       css:#id   xpath://a   text:Sign in   id:user-name   accessibility:Login   login.username
#  4. Use ${NAME} placeholders for secrets instead of typing them here.
#  Full sentence list: BDD.md

# ── WEB ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@ui @mysite
Feature: My web site

  @smoke
  Scenario: Search works
    Given the user opens the home page
    When the user types "shoes" into "css:input[name='q']"
    And the user presses the "ENTER" key
    Then the url should contain "search"
    And the user should see "text:Results"

# ── MOBILE (a separate feature file; tag @mobile + your app's site name, config in config/<site>.properties) ───
# @mobile @myapp
# Feature: My app
#   Scenario: Login
#     When the user types "demo" into "accessibility:username"
#     And the user taps "accessibility:login"
#     Then the user should see "text:Welcome"

# ── API (a separate feature file) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# @api @myapi
# Feature: My API
#   Scenario: Health
#     Given the user sets the header "Authorization" to "Bearer ${API_TOKEN}"
#     When the user sends a GET request to "/health"
#     Then the response status should be 200
